      "problem": "Calculate the points where the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=12$ intersects the line $x=-4.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=-4,$ we substitute $x=-4$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =12 \\\\ (-4)^{2}-{y}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 16-{y}^{2} & =12 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =12-16 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=-4$  at the points $(-4,\\pm 2).$",
